From 94996aeb9ceb3c0406830fe7a56c5e587bc7c061 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 22:41:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/24] fix(appstore): harden plugin installs with pnpm

The config directory keeps a pnpm-lock.yaml so that removing a plugin or
restoring the installed set works without the registry, and release age
gating is off so that a version installs as soon as it is published,
without pnpm writing a pnpm-workspace.yaml next to the settings. pnpm's
version and store are probed once: a missing pnpm is reported on every
platform, a pnpm older than 11 is refused, and a node_modules bound to
another store is rebuilt. Runs in the config directory are serialised,
since a settings restore ran alongside App Store installs and pnpm takes
no lock. The npm to pnpm migration pins the installed versions before
rebuilding node_modules so that plugins are not upgraded on the way,
recovers the backup of an interrupted rebuild, keeps that backup out of
settings backups, and renames the old tree out of the recovery path
before deleting it off the event loop, so that an interrupted deletion
is never taken for the user's installation. A leftover backup is told
apart by the manifest pnpm writes once every package is linked: beside
a completed rebuild it is dropped rather than restored over it. A
package that package.json does not declare is removed without pnpm,
which would refuse, and a dependency with a corrupt package.json no
longer stops the server from starting. The footer shows the pnpm
version and plugin-ci simulates the install with the same flags. It
counts a native addon as required when required dependencies alone
lead the plugin to it, following the installed manifests as Node
resolves them, so a version reached only optionally does not hide a
required one.

